            <ArticleTitle>Modification of 13X Molecular Sieve by Chitosan for Adsorptive Removal of Cadmium from Simulated Wastewater.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">E1101</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/ma10091101</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chitosan was used to modify a 13X molecular sieve to improve its cadmium removal capability. After being modified with 2% chitosan-acetate for 2 h at 30 °C, significant uptake of Cd2+ could be achieved. The uptake of Cd2+ on the modified 13X molecular sieve followed the Langmuir isotherms with a capacity of 1 mg/g. The kinetics of Cd2+ removal by modified 13X molecular sieve followed a pseudo second-order reaction, suggesting chemisorption or surface complexation. The Cd2+ removal with a sorbent dose of 2 g/L from an initial concentration of 100 μg/L reached more than 95% in 90 min. The equilibrium Cd2+ concentration was &lt;5 μg/L, which meets the requirements of &quot;Standards for Irrigation Water Quality&quot; (GB5084-2005) (10 μg/L) and MCL and MCLG for groundwater and drinking water (5 μg/L) set by United States Environmental Protection Agency.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
